From 4f9cb2d08a228861b6ce1453355efde2c55bc475 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Certe Kim <18364439+CerteKim@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:27:49 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sc8180x: add the APSS watchdog node

sc8180x is the one member of this SoC family whose device tree never got the
APSS (KPSS) watchdog described -- sc7180, sc7280, sc8280xp, sm8150 and sm8250
all have it at the same address, with the same clock and interrupt, and the
binding already lists "qcom,apss-wdt-sc8180x".  Nothing in EL1 made the
omission visible: Qualcomm's hypervisor (QHEE) owns EL2 and feeds the
bootloader-armed watchdog itself.

When Linux runs in EL2 instead (Secure-Launch via slbounce), nobody does, and
the machine resets itself roughly 30 seconds into the boot.  Describing the
device lets the qcom-wdt driver take it over: the driver marks the device
WDOG_HW_RUNNING and, with CONFIG_WATCHDOG_HANDLE_BOOT_ENABLED=y, the watchdog
core keeps pinging it until userspace opens /dev/watchdog.

Tested on the Xiaomi Book S 12.4 (sc8180x-xiaomi-book-12.4) both in EL1 and in
EL2.

Signed-off-by: Certe Kim <18364439+CerteKim@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x.dtsi | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x.dtsi
index f41f2bd..8819de8 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x.dtsi
@@ -4033,6 +4033,13 @@ apss_shared: mailbox@17c00000 {
 			#mbox-cells = <1>;
 		};
 
+		watchdog@17c10000 {
+			compatible = "qcom,apss-wdt-sc8180x", "qcom,kpss-wdt";
+			reg = <0x0 0x17c10000 0x0 0x1000>;
+			clocks = <&sleep_clk>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 0 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>;
+		};
+
 		timer@17c20000 {
 			compatible = "arm,armv7-timer-mem";
 			reg = <0x0 0x17c20000 0x0 0x1000>;
-- 
2.56.0